{"graph_chars": "Chapter Two opens with a fact that looks like free money. In two thousand twenty, among full-time workers, median weekly earnings were one thousand five hundred forty-five dollars with a master's degree, one thousand three hundred five with a bachelor's, and seven hundred eighty-one with a high school diploma. A bachelor's boosts earnings about sixty-seven percent over high school. A master's nearly doubles them. And yet in two thousand nineteen, only thirty-six percent of adults twenty-five and over had a bachelor's, and only thirteen point five percent had a master's. Why? Because every choice has a cost. Lionel Robbins wrote in nineteen thirty-two that the time at our disposal is limited. There are only twenty-four hours in the day. If we choose one thing we must relinquish others. Economists chuckled when the Rolling Stones recorded You Cannot Always Get What You Want, because they had been singing that tune for decades. Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative. The out-of-pocket costs of college include tuition, books, room, and board. The opportunity cost also includes lost earnings while you sit in class. After September eleventh, sky marshals might cost three billion a year, cockpit doors four hundred fifty million, scanners two billion. The single biggest cost of airport security is extra waiting time: about eight hundred million passengers, thirty extra minutes, valued at twenty dollars an hour, roughly eight billion dollars a year. Five extra dollars a day on lunch, times two hundred fifty workdays, is one thousand two hundred fifty dollars: a decent vacation. Name the cost as a vacation, and the choice may change.", "graph_times": [[0.0, 0.0601], [0.0601, 0.1202], [0.1202, 0.1804], [0.1804, 0.2405], [0.2405, 0.3006], [0.3006, 0.3607], [0.3607, 0.4208], [0.4208, 0.481], [0.481, 0.5411], [0.5411, 0.6012], [0.6012, 0.6613], [0.6613, 0.7214], [0.7214, 0.7816], [0.7816, 0.8417], [0.8417, 0.9018], [0.9018, 0.9619], [0.9619, 1